I kept the first version direct. For every starting index, the code keeps multiplying as the ending index moves forward. Every subarray product is checked, so negative values and zeros do not need special handling.

This is mainly useful for understanding the problem or testing a faster solution. It changes nothing in the input array.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        int best = nums[0];

        for (int start = 0; start < nums.length; start++) {
            int product = 1;

            for (int end = start; end < nums.length; end++) {
                product *= nums[end];
                best = Math.max(best, product);
            }
        }

        return best;
    }
}
```

For `nums = [2, 3, -2, 4]`, the products starting at index 0 are `2`, `6`, `-12`, and `-48`. The loop checks all other starting positions too, and keeps `6` as the answer.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A negative number can turn the smallest product into the largest one. Because of that, each position needs two values: the largest product ending there and the smallest product ending there.

When the current number is negative, these two values may switch roles. The code saves the old maximum before updating the pair. It only reads the array, so the input stays unchanged.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int maxProduct(vector<int>& nums) {
        int currentMax = nums[0];
        int currentMin = nums[0];
        int answer = nums[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            int value = nums[i];
            int oldMax = currentMax;

            currentMax = max(value, max({
                oldMax * value,
                currentMin * value
            }));
            currentMin = min(value, min({
                oldMax * value,
                currentMin * value
            }));

            answer = max(answer, currentMax);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[2, 3, -2, 4]`: after reading `2` and `3`, the best ending product is `6`. After `-2`, the minimum becomes `-12`, which is useful if another negative number appears later. The final answer is still `6`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Here the array is divided into two parts. The answer is either fully on the left, fully on the right, or crosses the middle. For a crossing product, the left side only needs its largest and smallest suffix products, and the right side needs the same prefix values.

This is longer than the one-pass method, but it shows another way to handle negative values. The vector is not changed.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    struct Info {
        long long total;
        long long prefixMax;
        long long prefixMin;
        long long suffixMax;
        long long suffixMin;
        long long best;
    };

    Info solve(const vector<int>& nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            long long value = nums[left];
            return {value, value, value, value, value, value};
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        Info first = solve(nums, left, middle);
        Info second = solve(nums, middle + 1, right);

        long long crossBest = max({
            first.suffixMax * second.prefixMax,
            first.suffixMax * second.prefixMin,
            first.suffixMin * second.prefixMax,
            first.suffixMin * second.prefixMin
        });

        long long prefixMax = max(
            first.prefixMax,
            first.total * second.prefixMax
        );
        long long prefixMin = min(
            first.prefixMin,
            first.total * second.prefixMin
        );

        long long suffixMax = max(
            second.suffixMax,
            second.total * first.suffixMax
        );
        long long suffixMin = min(
            second.suffixMin,
            second.total * first.suffixMin
        );

        return {
            first.total * second.total,
            prefixMax,
            prefixMin,
            suffixMax,
            suffixMin,
            max({first.best, second.best, crossBest})
        };
    }

public:
    int maxProduct(vector<int>& nums) {
        return static_cast<int>(solve(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1).best);
    }
};
```

A single element such as `[0]` reaches the base case and returns `0`. For `[2, -3, 4]`, the crossing candidates include `2 * -3 * 4 = -24`, along with smaller crossing pieces, so the best subarray is `[-3, 4]` with product `-12` or `[2]` with product `2`, depending on the values.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack

I split the array mentally at zeros. Inside a segment without zero, the product of all values is the best choice when the segment has an even number of negatives. With an odd number of negatives, removing either the prefix through the first negative or the suffix starting at the last negative gives the best positive part.

The code checks each zero-free segment and also handles a segment containing just one negative. It does not sort or change `nums`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        int answer = nums[0];
        int segmentStart = 0;

        while (segmentStart < nums.length) {
            if (nums[segmentStart] == 0) {
                answer = Math.max(answer, 0);
                segmentStart++;
                continue;
            }

            int segmentEnd = segmentStart;
            long totalProduct = 1;
            int negativeCount = 0;
            int firstNegative = -1;
            int lastNegative = -1;

            while (segmentEnd < nums.length && nums[segmentEnd] != 0) {
                totalProduct *= nums[segmentEnd];

                if (nums[segmentEnd] < 0) {
                    negativeCount++;
                    if (firstNegative == -1) {
                        firstNegative = segmentEnd;
                    }
                    lastNegative = segmentEnd;
                }

                segmentEnd++;
            }

            if (negativeCount % 2 == 0) {
                answer = Math.max(answer, (int) totalProduct);
            } else {
                long withoutPrefix = 1;
                for (int i = firstNegative + 1; i < segmentEnd; i++) {
                    withoutPrefix *= nums[i];
                }

                long withoutSuffix = 1;
                for (int i = segmentStart; i < lastNegative; i++) {
                    withoutSuffix *= nums[i];
                }

                answer = Math.max(answer, Math.max(
                    (int) withoutPrefix,
                    (int) withoutSuffix
                ));
            }

            segmentStart = segmentEnd;
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The zero case matters: for `[-2, 0, -1]`, the answer is `0`, not `-1`, because the single zero is a valid subarray. The input is only inspected.

The two extra loops over a negative segment still give `O(n)` total work, since each segment is processed a constant number of times.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

